WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.456
Benchmarks got you a shortlist.

2
00:00:01.456 --> 00:00:07.000
Now we build the thing that actually answers “which model wins for me” — a small eval set made of your own real cases.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.794
Library, Evals.

4
00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:12.450
A test case is two things: a prompt, and a description of what a good answer looks like.

5
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.000
Run the set against a model, and each case comes back scored against that description.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.704
Build three or four real cases — pulled from work you actually did this week, not invented ones.

7
00:00:20.704 --> 00:00:22.865
And include one where the right answer is “I don’t know.

8
00:00:22.865 --> 00:00:29.000
” A model that confidently invents an answer instead of admitting it lacks the information is a real failure mode, and it is the one benchmarks never test for.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:37.306
When to re-run this set: a new model shows up, a prompt or agent instruction changes, a provider quietly updates a model behind the same name, or you are just weighing a cheaper option.

10
00:00:37.306 --> 00:00:40.000
The set is cheap to keep — re-running it is the whole point.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.718
Reading the results honestly matters as much as building the set.

12
00:00:42.718 --> 00:00:45.436
A small score gap is noise — do not switch models over one point.

13
00:00:45.436 --> 00:00:47.652
Instead look at how a case fails, not just how often.

14
00:00:47.652 --> 00:00:52.000
And always weigh cost and speed alongside the score; the top scorer is not automatically the right pick.

15
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.112
Gotcha: a test case only proves what it tests.

16
00:00:54.112 --> 00:01:01.000
Four cases will not catch everything — but four real ones beat forty generic benchmark questions for telling you whether a model fits your actual job.

17
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.000
With Benchmarks and Evals covered, we turn to the app itself — a full walk through every settings panel, so nothing later needs re-explaining.

